LD1R/LD2R/LD3R/LD4R post-index -- decoders.

Companion to 0076, which did the plain replicate forms. All four post-index
variants (`LD<n>R_ASISDLSOP_R<n>_I`) were `return false` stubs, so they were
fatal `undecoded instruction`.

Measured 2026-09-01: with 0076 applied, a Go 1.26 hello-world got four
instructions further and stopped on

    1ef54: 4dffe804  ld4r {v4.4s-v7.4s}, [x0], #16
    1ef58: 4dffe808  ld4r {v8.4s-v11.4s}, [x0], #16

`RAPTORMARK_ECV_UNDEC_CENSUS=1` showed these were the LAST TWO distinct
undecoded addresses in the whole run -- with them skipped the binary reached the
512 MiB page-summary reservation and printed a normal traceback, the same place
`gosu` stops. So this patch is what completes Go 1.26 support, and enumerating
with the census beat chasing one fatal address per rebuild.

❗ SEMANTICS ARE SHARED with the plain forms and the ISELs point at the same
functions. The base-register update is not the semantic's job: `AddPostIndexMemOp`
carries it and the lifter applies it after the call, exactly as in 0072-0074.

⚠️ The access size stays ONE element while the advance is
`num_regs * elem_bytes` -- `ld4r {v4.4s-v7.4s}, [x0], #16` reads four 4-byte
elements and advances 16. Passing the total as the access size would have given
the memory operand a type the single-element semantics does not take.

Not covered: the register-offset replicate forms (`*_ASISDLSOP_RX<n>_R`,
i.e. `[Xn], Xm`). They keep their stubs, still fail loudly, and nothing measured
executes one.

